==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|congregatus}}, past participle of {{m|la|congregare||to congregate}}, from {{m|la|con-||with, together}} + {{m|la|gregare||to collect into a flock}}, from {{m|la|grex||flock, herd}}. See [[gregarious]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒŋ.ɡɹə.ɡeɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-congregate.wav |Audio (UK)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|rare}} [[collective|Collective]]; [[assembled]]; [[compact]].
#* '''1605''', {{w|Francis Bacon}}, ''The Advancement of Learning'', Book II, Chapter IX:
#*: With this reservation, therefore, we proceed to human philosophy or humanity, which hath two parts: the one considereth man segregate or distributively, the other '''congregate''' or in society; so as human philosophy is either simple and particular, or conjugate and civil.

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To collect into an [[assembly]] or [[assemblage]]; to bring into one place, or into a united body
#: {{syn|en|amass|assemble|compact|bring together|gather|mass|Thesaurus:round up}}
#* {{RQ:Hooker Laws}}
#*: Any multitude of Christian men '''congregated''' may be termed by the name of a church.
#* {{RQ:Coleridge Aids to Reflection}}
#*: Cold '''congregates''' all bodies.
#*{{RQ:Milton PL|book=7|passage=The great receptacle Of '''congregated''' waters he called Seas.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To come together; to assemble; to [[meet]].
#: {{syn|en|assemble|begather|forgather|Thesaurus:assemble}}
#*: {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|I|iii|passage=Even there where merchants most do '''congregate'''.}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|congregation}}

